1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:07,400
The leper fell to his knees at the town gate, his voice trembling, “Lord, if you are willing, you can make me clean.

2
00:00:07,350 --> 00:00:16,062
The crowd behind him fell silent, their eyes darting between the outcast and the man in the simple off‑white robe with a red mantle draped over one shoulder.

3
00:00:16,062 --> 00:00:20,325
Would the Son of God truly reach out to one they called unclean?

4
00:00:20,325 --> 00:00:26,925
Jesus had been walking the dusty road that led into Capernaum, his sandals stirring the grit beneath his feet.

5
00:00:26,925 --> 00:00:35,625
He moved with a calm that seemed to quiet the wind itself, the deep red mantle brushing the side of his robe as if it were a silent banner of compassion.

6
00:00:35,625 --> 00:00:46,162
The townspeople whispered as he passed, some curious, some fearful, for the Son of God had already spoken words that turned water into wine and driven merchants from the Temple.

7
00:00:46,162 --> 00:00:55,475
Yet no one expected him to pause at the edge of the city where the outcast waited, his skin marred by countless sores, his breath shallow with the weight of isolation.

8
00:00:55,475 --> 00:01:02,362
The leper had spent years behind the gate, barred from homes, from meals, from the simple touch of a hand.

9
00:01:02,362 --> 00:01:09,062
He had watched families gather at the market, heard the laughter of children, and felt the sting of being invisible.

10
00:01:09,062 --> 00:01:14,212
But the stories of the Messiah’s miracles had seeped into his heart like a hidden fire.

11
00:01:14,212 --> 00:01:19,350
When he saw Jesus approach, his hope surged, fragile yet fierce.

12
00:01:19,350 --> 00:01:27,762
He rose, each step a painful protest against the scarred flesh, and fell again, pressing his trembling hands to the hem of the mantle.

13
00:01:27,762 --> 00:01:34,350
Lord,” he whispered, the words breaking like thin glass, “if you are willing, you can make me clean.

14
00:01:34,350 --> 00:01:38,775
Jesus looked down, his kind dark eyes meeting the leper’s.

15
00:01:38,775 --> 00:01:45,337
In that instant the world seemed to narrow to the two of them, the clamor of the gate fading into a distant hum.

16
00:01:45,337 --> 00:01:53,087
He reached out, his fingers brushing the leper’s arm, and the crowd gasped—no one had ever seen the Messiah touch a leper.

17
00:01:53,087 --> 00:01:58,350
I am willing,” Jesus said, his voice steady as the tide, “be clean.

18
00:01:58,350 --> 00:02:07,575
A warm light seemed to flicker around the touch, and the sores that had marred the man's skin began to dissolve, the rawness fading like mist under sunrise.

19
00:02:07,575 --> 00:02:13,100
The leper’s breath caught, a gasp of disbelief, as his body felt whole again.

20
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:19,875
He stood, his limbs no longer trembling with pain, his eyes wide with a mixture of awe and tears.

21
00:02:19,875 --> 00:02:24,862
The crowd surged forward, a wave of murmurs and startled exclamences.

22
00:02:24,862 --> 00:02:32,025
Some fell to their knees, praising God; others stared, unsure whether to believe what they had just witnessed.

23
00:02:32,025 --> 00:02:39,550
The priests who had been warned against touching lepers watched from a distance, their faces hidden beneath the shadows of the gate’s arches.

24
00:02:39,550 --> 00:02:49,987
Yet even as the leper lifted his hands in gratitude, a new commotion rose from within the town’s narrow streets—a thudding sound, a hurried shout, a roof being lifted.

25
00:02:49,987 --> 00:02:55,450
What was happening beyond the gate, and why did Jesus’ gaze shift so quickly toward it?

26
00:02:55,612 --> 00:03:03,024
The house in Capernaum was already packed, a sea of heads and breath pressed together as though the walls themselves might give way.

27
00:03:02,974 --> 00:03:10,299
The low ceiling of the upper room swayed under the hurried hands of four friends, their faces set in grim determination.

28
00:03:10,299 --> 00:03:19,162
They had come carrying a man on a simple mat, his body a bundle of limp limbs, his eyes clouded with the ache of many days spent unable to move.

29
00:03:19,162 --> 00:03:26,574
The crowd at the doorway pressed forward, the murmurs rising like wind through reeds, but the friends would not be turned aside.

30
00:03:26,574 --> 00:03:33,587
With a collective grunt they lifted the heavy tiles of the roof, the wooden beams groaning as ancient nails gave way.

31
00:03:33,587 --> 00:03:44,574
The opening yawned, a dark aperture that seemed to swallow the noise, and then, with a careful tilt, the mat was lowered, the paralytic descending into the midst of the waiting throng.

32
00:03:44,574 --> 00:03:55,637
A gasp escaped the onlookers as the man’s limp form hung briefly in the air, suspended between the heavens and the earth, and all eyes fixed on the frail figure that now lay at Jesus’ feet.

33
00:03:55,637 --> 00:04:08,099
Yet even as the crowd inhaled the shock of the sight, a subtle tremor ran through the room—an unspoken question that hung in the dust: would the faith that had lowered him be enough to draw the Master’s power into this broken body?

34
00:04:08,099 --> 00:04:15,662
Jesus stood, his mantle draped over one shoulder, the red fabric catching the dim light that filtered through the roof opening.

35
00:04:15,662 --> 00:04:23,024
He looked not at the man’s trembling limbs but at the faces of those who had brought him, at the desperate hope that shone in their eyes.

36
00:04:23,024 --> 00:04:31,687
Your sins are forgiven,” He said, his voice resonating through the packed room, a declaration that seemed to cut through the clamor like a blade of wind.

37
00:04:31,687 --> 00:04:36,762
The words fell heavy, and a ripple of surprise surged through the gathered crowd.

38
00:04:36,762 --> 00:04:44,924
From the back of the assembly, a group of teachers of the law—a few stern‑faced scribes—shifted, their brows furrowing.

39
00:04:44,924 --> 00:04:51,812
Who can forgive sins but God alone?” one whispered, his tone a mixture of curiosity and accusation.

40
00:04:51,812 --> 00:05:00,724
The friends, still clutching the edges of the mat, exchanged uneasy glances, their faith suddenly caught in a web of theological doubt.

41
00:05:00,724 --> 00:05:08,987
The paralytic, eyes wide and breath shallow, seemed to sense the tension, his body still as stone even as his spirit trembled.

42
00:05:08,987 --> 00:05:18,624
As the murmurs grew louder, a silent challenge rose among the teachers: could this man truly speak on behalf of God, or would his words betray a hidden agenda?

43
00:05:18,624 --> 00:05:23,787
The room waited, the weight of the question pressing down like the very beams above them.

44
00:05:23,787 --> 00:05:29,374
The scribes, emboldened by their own authority, began to voice their discontent.

45
00:05:29,374 --> 00:05:34,749
We know what the law says,” one declared, “that blasphemy is a grave offense.

46
00:05:34,749 --> 00:05:39,962
If He claims to forgive sins, He is daring to place Himself above the Almighty.

47
00:05:39,962 --> 00:05:44,737
Their words crackled, a storm of doctrine threatening to eclipse the moment.

48
00:05:44,737 --> 00:05:50,224
Jesus turned his gaze toward them, his dark eyes steady, his calm unshaken.

49
00:05:50,224 --> 00:05:58,862
Which is easier,” He asked, his voice gentle yet firm, “to say, ‘Your sins are forgiven,’ or to say, ‘Rise and walk’?

50
00:05:58,862 --> 00:06:05,862
But that you may know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins, I will also do something that will prove it.

51
00:06:05,862 --> 00:06:10,974
The crowd leaned in, the suspense coiling around them like a rope about to snap.

52
00:06:10,974 --> 00:06:17,399
The friends tightened their grip on the mat, the paralytic’s hand brushing the fabric as if feeling for a sign.

53
00:06:17,399 --> 00:06:28,537
In that instant, a subtle shift seemed to pass through the room, a breath held in anticipation—would the Master speak through the sick man, or would He turn his power upon the teachers themselves?

54
00:06:28,708 --> 00:06:33,470
With a sudden motion, Jesus extended his hand toward the man on the mat.

55
00:06:33,420 --> 00:06:39,758
Take up your mat and go home,” He commanded, his voice ringing clear over the shuffling of the crowd.

56
00:06:39,758 --> 00:06:44,620
The words were simple, yet they carried a weight that seemed to bend the very air.

57
00:06:44,620 --> 00:06:49,533
The paralytic’s eyes flickered, a spark of recognition igniting within them.

58
00:06:49,533 --> 00:06:57,295
He pushed against the mat, his fingers finding purchase, and then, as if a hidden spring had been released, his legs obeyed.

59
00:06:57,295 --> 00:07:02,683
The wood of the floor creaked beneath him, the sound echoing like a drumbeat of triumph.

60
00:07:02,683 --> 00:07:08,858
He rose, his body steadier with each step, the mat now a banner of his newfound strength.

61
00:07:08,858 --> 00:07:17,133
The crowd erupted, a cacophony of astonishment and praise, the scribes momentarily silenced by the undeniable miracle before them.

62
00:07:17,133 --> 00:07:25,933
Yet even as the man walked toward the doorway, a new murmur rose among the spectators: “Who is this man that commands even the very laws of nature?

63
00:07:25,933 --> 00:07:32,045
The question hung in the air, a seed that would sprout into deeper inquiry about the source of such power.

64
00:07:32,045 --> 00:07:38,008
As the paralytic disappeared from view, the house buzzed with a mixture of awe and unease.

65
00:07:38,008 --> 00:07:45,745
Some shouted, “Truly, He is a prophet!” while others, still wary, whispered, “What does this mean for the law?

66
00:07:45,745 --> 00:07:54,108
The teachers of the law, their faces flushed with a mixture of anger and curiosity, stepped forward, their eyes fixed on Jesus.

67
00:07:54,108 --> 00:07:59,495
If you are the Son of God,” one demanded, “why do you break the Sabbath by healing?

68
00:07:59,495 --> 00:08:03,220
Why do you claim authority that belongs only to the Father?

69
00:08:03,220 --> 00:08:09,433
Their words cut the lingering reverence, a sharp edge that threatened to undo the miracle’s impact.

70
00:08:09,433 --> 00:08:17,795
Jesus met their challenge without hesitation, his gaze steady, his mantle rustling softly as he turned his head toward the street outside.

71
00:08:17,795 --> 00:08:28,070
There, framed by the doorway, stood a man in a fine blue robe, his hands counting coins at a tax booth, his eyes lifting as if caught by an unseen current.

72
00:08:28,070 --> 00:08:35,420
The scene held a promise of something yet to be revealed—could this orderly collector be the next to hear the call that would change his life?

73
00:08:35,420 --> 00:08:43,908
The question lingered, the crowd’s breath held, as the episode drifted toward a new horizon, waiting for the next voice to break the silence.

74
00:08:43,908 --> 00:08:53,720
Jesus stepped out of the dim, crowded house, the air of the open street pressing against his face, the scent of salt and fish mingling with the sharp scent of the marketplace.

75
00:08:53,720 --> 00:09:04,383
The city of Capernaum had, for a moment, forgotten its ordinary rhythm; the crowd's murmur had faded, replaced by a hush that settled like a veil over the cobblestones.

76
00:09:04,383 --> 00:09:15,933
He walked slowly, each footfall resonating in the quiet, and his eyes caught a familiar shape—a man in a fine blue robe, his hands busy with the steady counting of coins at a tax booth.

77
00:09:15,933 --> 00:09:26,308
The man, Matthew, had been a tax collector, a figure of authority in the eyes of the people, yet his gaze lifted, startled, as if a sudden wind had brushed against him.

78
00:09:26,308 --> 00:09:30,258
Jesus felt the pull of purpose tighten around his shoulders.

79
00:09:30,258 --> 00:09:35,533
The world seemed to hold its breath, as if waiting for a word that could change everything.

80
00:09:35,533 --> 00:09:42,483
Yet the real invitation had not yet been spoken, and the silence hung heavy, a promise waiting to be fulfilled.

81
00:09:42,483 --> 00:09:48,345
The disciple approached with the calm certainty of a man who had walked through a storm and found the shore.

82
00:09:48,345 --> 00:09:54,195
Matthew looked up, his eyes flicking between the coins and the man who had walked into his life.

83
00:09:54,195 --> 00:09:59,220
Jesus' voice, gentle yet firm, broke the quiet: "Follow me.

84
00:09:59,220 --> 00:10:05,395
The words were simple, but their weight was immense, a call that echoed in the depths of Matthew's heart.

85
00:10:05,395 --> 00:10:14,333
He had spent his days counting the weight of the world on his shoulders, and now, for the first time, his own weight seemed to shift toward a different direction.

86
00:10:14,333 --> 00:10:23,258
Yet Matthew's heart trembled, unsure of the cost of leaving the safety of the tax booth, of the familiar rhythm that had defined his life for years.

87
00:10:23,258 --> 00:10:31,795
He felt the weight of the eyes of his brethren, the whispers of the townsfolk, and the unspoken fear of what would become of his family and his livelihood.

88
00:10:31,795 --> 00:10:36,395
He did not yet know what lay beyond that single, simple sentence.

89
00:10:36,556 --> 00:10:46,618
In a sudden, almost involuntary motion, Matthew reached out, his fingers brushing the rough leather of the mat that had carried the paralytic to the threshold of Jesus' house.

90
00:10:46,568 --> 00:10:51,931
The touch was a promise, a silent agreement that he would leave the life he knew behind.

91
00:10:51,931 --> 00:10:57,106
The very air seemed to thicken, as if the world itself were waiting for his decision.

92
00:10:57,106 --> 00:11:04,043
The price of leaving was higher than he imagined; it was a leap into an unknown that would test his faith and his resolve.

93
00:11:04,043 --> 00:11:12,193
The sound of coins clinking against the stone echoed in his ears, a reminder of the life he had built, the responsibilities he carried.

94
00:11:12,193 --> 00:11:19,506
Yet, as he set down his counting tools, he felt the presence of something far greater than the weight of a taxman's ledger.

95
00:11:19,506 --> 00:11:27,968
He was no longer bound by the strictures of his former occupation; the call to follow was a path that would lead him beyond the borders of his known world.

96
00:11:27,968 --> 00:11:35,531
He had to decide whether to let the world that had known him for years slip away, or to answer a call that promised a different destiny.

97
00:11:35,531 --> 00:11:42,956
The street was alive with the clatter of carts, the chatter of merchants, the distant cries of a hawker selling fresh fish.

98
00:11:42,956 --> 00:11:48,318
The sun, now higher in the sky, bathed the cobblestones in a golden glow.

99
00:11:48,318 --> 00:11:54,518
Matthew lifted his gaze, eyes flicking back and forth between Jesus and the bustling market.

100
00:11:54,518 --> 00:12:05,743
He had heard the stories—how the Messiah healed the blind, the sick, the leprous—and now, in his own presence, the possibility of being part of that miracle was tangible.

101
00:12:05,743 --> 00:12:12,393
He understood that the path ahead was unclear, yet a promise shone brighter than any coin he had ever counted.

102
00:12:12,393 --> 00:12:17,943
He could feel the warmth of the sun on his cheek and the cool breeze that seemed to whisper his name.

103
00:12:17,943 --> 00:12:24,243
It was a moment where the ordinary met the extraordinary, where a life of counting could become a life of following.

104
00:12:24,243 --> 00:12:34,443
With a slow breath that steadied his trembling, Matthew stepped forward, his hands still clutching the last coin, the last weight of his past, and turned to leave the booth behind.

105
00:12:34,443 --> 00:12:42,918
He felt the pulse of a new rhythm, a heartbeat that matched the rhythm of the city and the promise of a future that could not be measured in silver or gold.

106
00:12:42,918 --> 00:12:46,718
The decision, once made, could not be undone.

107
00:12:46,718 --> 00:12:54,243
The crowd that had gathered in the house now dispersed into the streets, their voices a soft background hum as Matthew walked away.

108
00:12:54,243 --> 00:13:00,618
Jesus watched him, a quiet smile crossing his face, as if he had known the outcome all along.

109
00:13:00,618 --> 00:13:09,531
He had seen the transformation in the eyes of the man he had healed, the hope in the face of the paralytic, and now he saw the spark of faith ignite in Matthew.

110
00:13:09,531 --> 00:13:17,793
The scene closed on the image of Matthew's silhouette fading into the bustling market, the blue robe fluttering like a banner of new beginnings.

111
00:13:17,793 --> 00:13:24,993
The moment hung, a gentle reminder that the call had been answered, and the world had shifted, one step at a time.

112
00:13:24,993 --> 00:13:32,793
But the real test had not even begun, as Jesus' gaze lingered on the horizon, where the city’s life would continue to unfold.

113
00:13:32,793 --> 00:13:34,431
Next time…

114
00:13:34,431 --> 00:13:36,281
God bless you.

